// §11.9 pinned symbols (#1396) — the mirror of `extern function`: §11.7
// CONSUMES an external symbol, §11.9 PRODUCES one.
//
// `modulePrefix` prefixes every non-root module's symbols with `m<id>$`, where
// `<id>` is assigned by whichever build imports the module. Code generation
// calls the runtime by fixed name (`bit_rt_alloc`, `bit_rt_safepoint`, ... —
// runtime/ABI.md), so until a declaration could pin its emitted name, a runtime
// written in Bit could never define the symbols the compiler calls. This
// program is that pin, exercised as a real linked binary.
//
// The proof is the LINK, not the arithmetic. `bit_pinned_add` below is declared
// `extern` — the compiler emits an undefined reference to that exact byte
// string, knowing nothing about `rt.pinnedAdd`. The linker binds it to the
// pinned definition in `./rt`, so:
//
//   (a) a call through the raw name returns 42 — the symbol resolved, and to
//       the right function body. The callee subtracts, so this also pins down
//       argument ORDER across the C ABI, which a commutative operation would
//       let slide;
//   (b) `plainSub`, the unpinned control in the SAME module, still works
//       through the ordinary `m<id>$`-prefixed path;
//   (c) `nm -u` on this binary lists NO undefined `_bit_pinned_sub`: the
//       reference was satisfied locally by the pinned definition. Change the
//       pinned name by one character, or drop the pin, and that symbol becomes
//       an unresolved libSystem import instead — dyld then aborts the program
//       at load with `Symbol not found: _bit_pinned_sub` (exit 134). That is
//       what makes (a) non-vacuous.
//
// Runs on every target: `extern function` (§11.7) admits a symbol this build's
// own modules pin via §11.9 on Linux exactly as it does on Darwin — a static
// ELF link resolves the reference out of the program's own object, the same
// way it resolves `bit_main`. The pin itself is target-independent regardless:
// it is applied during lowering, before any code generation, and is verified on
// aarch64-macos, aarch64-linux and x86_64-linux by a separate probe recorded on
// #1396.
import { plainSub } from "./rt"

// Resolved at link time, purely by name, against `./rt`'s pinned definition.
extern function bit_pinned_sub(a: i64, b: i64): i64

function main() {
  println("${bit_pinned_sub(64, 22)} ${plainSub(50, 8)}")
}
